  <content>
    <section class="practice">
      <exercise id="b491d30f-059b-4964-a89b-9781c582952c" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem>
          <para>Translate to an equation: Which equation means nine more than x is equal to 52?</para>
          <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
          <para>\(x+9=52\)</para>
          <para>\(9x=52\)</para>
          <para>\(9-x=52\)</para>
          <para>\(\frac{52}x=9\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.2.3.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is \(x\) + 9 = 52.</para>
          <para>Nine more than \(x\) means that 9 is added to \(x\). \(x+9\) \(x+9\) Equal to 52
            means that we take whatever expression that is in front of 52 and set them equal to each
            other. Equal to 52 means that we take whatever expression that is in front of 52 and set
            them equal to each other. \(x+9=52\) \(x+9=52\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_22b3e07c-7dc1-4a38-b608-7a666569a4cd" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem>
          <para>Translate to an equation: Which equation means ten less than \(m\) is −14?</para>
          <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
          <para>\(10-m=-14\)</para>
          <para>\(m-10=14\)</para>
          <para>\(m-10=-14\)</para>
          <para>\(-14-m=10\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.2.3.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is \(m− 10 = −14\).</para>
          <para>Ten less than \(m\) means that 10 is subtracted from \(m\). \(m-10\) \(m-10\) The
            word is, is the same as “equal to”. This means we take whatever expression that is in
            front of -14 and set them equal to each other. The word is, is the same as “equal to”.
            This means we take whatever expression that is in front of -14 and set them equal to
            each other. \(m-10=-14\) \(m-10=-14\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="abd594a7-8217-4e2f-b396-f65eedf94e00" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem>
          <para>Translate to an equation: Which equation means the sum of \(y\) and −30 is 40?</para>
          <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
          <para>\(-30y=40\)</para>
          <para>\(y= 40+(-30)\)</para>
          <para>\(y+40=-30\)</para>
          <para>\(y+(-30)=40\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.2.3.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is \(y + (−30) = 40\).</para>
          <para>The sum of \(y\) and -30 means that you add them together. \(y+(-30)\) \(y+(-30)\)
            The word is, is the same as “equal to”. This means we take whatever expression that is
            in front of 40 and set them equal to each other. The word is, is the same as “equal to”.
            This means we take whatever expression that is in front of 40 and set them equal to each
            other.</para><para>\(y+(-30)=40\) \(y+(-30)=40\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="d3f034b8-6d94-4cda-8653-d0ea84422be3" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem>
          <para>Translate to an equation: For a family birthday dinner, Celeste bought a turkey that
            weighed 5 pounds less than the one she bought for Thanksgiving. The birthday turkey
            weighed 16 pounds. Which equation represents how much the Thanksgiving turkey weighed?</para>
          <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
          <para>\(5-x=16\)</para>
          <para>\(x-5=16\)</para>
          <para>\(x+5=16\)</para>
          <para>\(-x+5=16\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.2.3.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is \(x − 5 = 16\).</para>
          <para>The important facts from the problem are that the weight of the birthday turkey is
            16 pounds and that it is 5 pounds less than the Thanksgiving turkey. Choose a variable,
            \(x\), to represent the Thanksgiving turkey. Choose a variable, \(x\), to represent the
            Thanksgiving turkey. The birthday turkey was 5 pounds less than the one she bought for
            Thanksgiving. The birthday turkey was 5 pounds less than the one she bought for
            Thanksgiving. \(x-5\) \(x-5\) We also know the birthday turkey is 16 pounds. So they can
            be set equal to each other. We also know the birthday turkey is 16 pounds. So they can
            be set equal to each other. \(x-5=16\) \(x-5=16\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="a1940752-9c3b-4a6b-b93c-0c93867eb450" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem>
          <para>Translate to an equation: Which equation represents 45% of 120?</para>
          <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
          <para>\(x=45(120)\)</para>
          <para>\(x=4.5(120)\)</para>
          <para>\(x=0.45(120)\)</para>
          <para>\(x=45+120\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.2.4.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is \(x = 0.45(120)\).</para>
          <para>45% is equivalent to \(\frac{45}{100}\) or 0.45. The word “of” means that you need
            to multiply the two numbers together. The word “of” means that you need to multiply the
            two numbers together. .45 times 120 or \(0.45(120)\) .45 times 120 or \(0.45(120)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_0cbf272c-f4ba-47be-b722-0c6b17eb0acb" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem>
          <para>Translate to an equation: 250% of 65 can also be written as:</para>
          <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
          <para>\(x=2.5(65)\)</para>
          <para>\(x=0.25(65)\)</para>
          <para>\(x=25 (65)\)</para>
          <para>\(x=250(65)\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.2.4.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is x = 2.5(65).</para>
          <para>250% is equivalent to \(\frac{250}{100}\) or 2.5 \(\frac{250}{100}\) or 2.5 The word
            “of” means that you need to multiply the two numbers together. The word “of” means that
            you need to multiply the two numbers together. 2.5 times 65 or \(2.5(65)\) 2.5 times 65
            or \(2.5(65)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_4741cdce-5459-483b-9ce8-6a65c3a273db" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem>
          <para>Translate to an equation: Geneva treated her parents to dinner at their favorite
            restaurant. The bill was $74.25. Geneva wants to leave 16% of the total bill as a tip.
            How much should the tip be?</para>
          <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
          <para>\(t=16(74.25)\)</para>
          <para>\(t = 0.16(74.25)\)</para>
          <para>\(t = 1.6(74.25)\)</para>
          <para>\(t =16+(74.25)\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.2.4.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is \(t = 0.16(74.25)\).</para>
          <para>The tip is a percentage of the bill so 16% of 74.25. 16% is equivalent to 16% is
            equivalent to \(\frac{16}{100}\) or 0.16 \(\frac{16}{100}\) or 0.16 The word “of” means
            that you need to multiply the two numbers together. The word “of” means that you need to
            multiply the two numbers together. 0.16 times 74.25 or \(0.16(74.25)\) 0.16 times 74.25
            or \(0.16(74.25)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="dc4e400d-0e2a-4fbd-b9bb-6098f4f99c4b" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem>
          <para>Large cheese pizzas cost $5 each, and large one-topping pizzas cost $6 each. Choose
            the equation that represents the total cost, \(T\), of \(c\) large cheese pizzas and
            \(d\) large one-topping pizzas.</para>
          <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
          <para>\(T=6c+5d\)</para>
          <para>\(T=5c+6d\)</para>
          <para>\(T=5c-6d\)</para>
          <para>\(T=6c-5d\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.2.3.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is \(T=5c+6d\).</para>
          <para>Our equation in words would be that total cost is going to equal (the cost of one
            cheese pizza times the number of cheese pizzas purchased) plus (the cost of one
            one-topping pizza times the number of one-topping pizzas purchased).</para>
          <para>Substitute in the variables and prices that are given in the problem. \(T=5c+6d\)
            \(T=5c+6d\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_857c230c-f428-4b8a-8086-fa1a7d82df3f" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem>
          <para>Jada plans to serve milk and healthy cookies for a book club meeting. She is
            preparing 12 ounces of milk and 4 cookies per person. Including herself, there are 15
            people in the club. A package of cookies contains 24 cookies and costs $4.50. A 1-gallon
            jug of milk contains 128 ounces and costs $3. Let \( n \) represent the number of people
            in the club, \(m\) represent the ounces of milk, \(c\) represent the number of cookies,
            and \(b\) represent Jada’s budget in dollars.</para>
          <para>Select<emphasis>three</emphasis> of the equations that could represent the
            quantities and constraints in this situation.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.2.3.</para>
          <para>The correct answers are: \(m=12(15)\), \(4n=c\), and \(b=2(3)+3(4.50)\).</para>
          <para>The three equations that could represent the quantities and constraints in this
            situation are:</para>
          <para>\(m=12(15)\), represents the amount of milk that needs to be purchased is the amount
            each person will receive multiplied by the number of people in the club. This solution
            only works if there are 15 people in the club.</para>
          <para>\(4n=c\), represents the number of cookies that each person will receive times the
            variable \(n\) which represents the number of people in the club equals the number of
            cookies that need to be purchased. This solution can work for any amount of club
            members.</para>
          <para>\(b=2(3)+3(4.50)\), represents Jada’s budget. The number of 1-gallon jugs of milk
            she needs times the cost of each jug, 2(3). Plus the number of packages of cookies she
            needs times the cost of each package, 3(4.50). She needs 12(15)=180 ounces of milk. She
            will purchase \(\frac{180}{128}=1.41\) gallon jugs, but since she can’t buy .41 of a
            jug, she will have to buy 2 of them at $3.00 each. . She needs 4(15)=60 cookies. She has
            to purchase \(\frac{60}{24}=2.5\) packages, but since she can‚t buy .5 of a package, she
            will have to buy 3 of them at $4.50 each. This solution can only work if she does not
            need more that 180 ounces of milk or 60 cookies and if the jugs of milk cost exactly
            $3.00 and the packages of cookies cost exactly $4.50.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
